.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Jul 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-auth-login - Sign in with your Ethereum wallet (SIWE)


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 auth login [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Authenticates with the RAIL0 gateway using Sign-In with Ethereum (EIP-4361).
.IP "  1." 5
Fetches a single-use nonce from the API
.IP "  2." 5
Builds a SIWE message and signs it with your private key
.IP "  3." 5
Verifies the signature and stores the JWT token

.PP
Any wallet can sign in — a gateway account is NOT required. Signing proves
control of the address, which is enough to read your own address-scoped
resources (e.g. 'payments list' shows every payment where you are payer or
payee). A gateway account only unlocks merchant-scoped features.

.PP
The token is saved to ~/.cache/rail0/token and used automatically by all
commands that require authentication (e.g. 'payments list').
Override with the RAIL0_TOKEN environment variable.

.PP
The signing key is resolved like every other signing command: pass -p as a raw
0x-prefixed hex key, as @name for a key saved in the OS keychain ('rail0 keys
add'), or omit it to fall back to the RAIL0_PRIVATE_KEY environment variable.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for login

.PP
\fB-p\fP, \fB--private-key\fP=""
	Signing key: 0x-prefixed hex, @name for an OS-keychain key ('rail0 keys add'), or RAIL0_PRIVATE_KEY


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 auth login -p @payee     # or -p 0xYourPrivateKey…
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-auth(1)\fP
